 "asr_text": "The Federal Reserve raised interest rates, and its own interest bill went up. In September, the Fed raised rates by a quarter point. Here's the problem. Banks keep roughly $3 trillion in reserves at the Fed, and the Fed pays them interest. Just that quarter point increase would add about $7.5 billion a year in interest costs, if reserves stayed the same. But much of the Fed's bond income doesn't rise as quickly. When expenses outweigh income, losses build into what's called a deferred asset. By early October, that balance was roughly $234 billion. Until future earnings cover it, the Fed's normal payments to the Treasury stay paused. In 2021, those payments were more than $107 billion. Higher rates can fight inflation. But they can also make the Fed's own finances harder to repair.",
 "comparison": {
